# Beyond powders: Monoliths on the basis of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)

Review
## A R T I C L E  I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Keywords:   
Metal-organic frameworks   
Monolith   
3D printing   
Motor
In the past two decades, considerable metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been identified and prepared, showing great promise in various fields. In general, the powdery MOFs have limited mechanical strength and poor processability and recyclability owing to the rigidity and fragility of MOFs. Moreover, the cost ease and large-scale production with good controllability and integrity are prerequisites to cross the gap from lab to market, especially for large-scale application. Most recently, the fabrication of MOF-based 3D monoliths is of greatest focus to tackle the aforementioned issues. Herein, we briefly summarize the representative fabrication strategies and features of 3D MOF-monoliths and their application in energy storage, separation, gas storage, biomedical applications, catalysis and motors. The fabricated MOF-monoliths exhibit excellent advantages and even additional bonus to surpass MOF powder in different aspects, including: 1) improved robustness benefited from substrates and 3D structures; 2) enhanced reusability facilitated by integrity and large-salacity; 3) extra pores and active sites for accommodating more guest molecules; 4) ease in medical supplies preparation with enhanced processabilities. Thus, MOF-monoliths have promising advantages with good commercial-transnationality and clinical-transnationality.
## 1. Introduction
Since the considerable diversity of porous materials, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are emerging as prospective materials with explo­ sive research attention. MOFs, also denoted as porous coordinative polymers, are a class of hybrid crystalline materials, and the inherent porosity is obtained by highly ordered 3-dimentioanl (3D) structures in which metal ions coordinated with the bridging organic nodes [1]. In the past three decades, MOF-related area has experienced explosive devel­ opment in novel MOFs synthesis, underlying mechanism exploration and ever-expanding scope of applications [2–3]. The tremendous vari­ ability of organic and inorganic linkers renders MOFs topological di­ versity and structural adjustability. In addition, benefiting from their topological diversity, extraordinarily high surface area and tunable pores or channels, MOFs have been engineered in various domains, including energy storage [4–5], heterogeneous catalysis [6–7], biomedicine [8–9], separation [10–11], et c. Moreover, MOFs are not only restricted at the laboratory level, but also has been promoted to commercialization. For example, ION-X™ from NuMat Technologies, is the first product to integrate MOFs for gas storage [12]. The collabo­ ration from Yaghi group and BSAF is promoting the development of natural gas-fueled trucks [13].
Despite the exciting progress on market, verifying the great potential of MOFs in both science and technology, there are still a lot of chal­ lenges. One big issue is that MOFs are generally harvested in the poly­ disperse microcrystalline powder form, making them difficult to be processed, integrated and recycled owing to the rigidity and fragility of MOFs [14–15]. Especially in the field of industry catalysis, MOF pow­ ders as catalysts are difficult to be recycled, resulting in high cost [16–17]. Furthermore, according to the United States Department of Energy (DOE) 2015, $0.09\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{H}_{2}{\bullet}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ or $0.081\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{H}_{2}{\bullet}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ are set to be the minimal on-board requirement [18], requiring high-density gas storage, which is difficult to be reached by MOF powder as large amount of void space exists with dramatically decreased volumetric adsorption capacities. Although high surface area is one of the most outstanding features of MOFs, the ultra-high adsorption capacities and energy den­ sity in perspective of the gravimetric and volumetric dimension is also of great importance, even possessing a higher priority. The cost ease and large-scale production are prerequisites to cross the gap from lab to market.
To tackle aforementioned issues, the manifold integration of multi­ form functions into a single MOF-based monolith with visible 3D ar­ chitecture, has become highly envisioned and the advancement and progress researchers made promote clinical and industrial translation in the near future. Recently, porous materials-based robotic system and advanced 3D architecture are performing eye-catchingly in various do­ mains, to break the bottleneck of assembling MOFs into a monolith with higher packing density, enhanced robustness and structural continuity.
As most applications of MOFs are facilitated by extremely high sur­ face area and inherent pores or channels in MOFs, the fabrication of MOF-mediated monolith should make a good combination of advan­ tages derived from both integration and structural properties. Enhanced mechanical stability can be obtained from the 3D architecture with good scalability and reusability for large-scale applications. Generally, com­ parable surface area and porosity of MOFs are retained with inherent chemical and physical functionalities when fabricated as an integrated monolith, in order to accomplish assigned tasks. More excitingly, the monolithic architecture gains even better performance than MOF pow­ der. Therefore, the MOF-mediated monoliths pave the way to address drawbacks of MOFs and improve related properties as well, targeting feasible translation in the near future with goals including: (i) easier processability with improved structural rigidity and mechanical stabil­ ity, (ii) more mass loading and lower mass transfer resistance, (iii) enhanced reusability and (iv) and higher volumetric adsorption capacities.
Initially, progress was made on MOF-based thin film [19–21] and fiber [22–23]. Strictly speaking, they should be classified as 2D mate­ rials, rather than 3D architecture, so those won’t be discussed deeply in this review. Here, we focus on MOF monoliths in the form of a whole architecture integrating functions from each component. In most cases, both MOF and the substrate contribute in the related application, which is the most typical and significant difference compared with 2D
membranes or fibers.
So far, in the limited number of published articles about MOF monolith, a review article on MOF monoliths was published by Hou et al. [14] focused mainly on MOF gels; a few reviews put emphasis on 3D printing techniques for fabrication of MOF-based monolith in various applications, but other fabrication strategies are not included [24–25]; another review elaborated on the strategies based on some specific ap­ plications, such as separation and gas storage [13]. Overall, a compre­ hensive review of 3D MOF monoliths with systematic investigation on both fabrication strategies and related applications based on a detailed discussion is still lacking. The scope of this review is to summarize the cutting-edge progress in 3D MOF monoliths with critical comments in both fabrication strategies and applications.
## 2. Fabrication strategies
Generally, MOFs are synthesized in bulk crystalline powder form, which is difficult to recycle, integrate and processing. Reaching onindustry scale is necessary to leap the gap from laboratory to commer­ cialization, especially for large-scale application. In this part, we will introduce fabrication methods for building MOF-based monoliths as illustrated in Fig. 1 and Table 1, with good summary and critical comments.
## 2.1. 3D printing
Palletization is one of feasible synthesis methods, but often leads to decrease in surface area and functionality [26]. For MOFs, the periodical porosity and high surface area should be retained in order to keep applicable advantages and desirable properties benefitted from natural pores of MOFs. More importantly, the accessible pores should be guar­ anteed to accomplish pore-facilitating applications. Generally, 3D printing is a waste-avoiding route with designable structure, which is normally precisely maneuvered by automatic control system and bene­ ficial to small batch production [27]. 3D printing technique is one of the most promising strategies for industrial scaling up with unique advan­ tages in considerable application scenarios with excellent extendibility and designability.
The prototype is designed by a computer-aided design (CAD) soft­ ware (i.e. SolidWorks Corporation) as a 3D model, which is then sliced into cross-sectional layers. The printable ink for fabricating MOF-based monoliths is usually hydrogel [26]. Afterwards, a control system is applied at appropriate mechanical pressure and printing speed during 3D printing. An advanced strategy based on 3D printing was developed by Lim et al., [26] and the resultant 3D printed HKUST-1 monolith maintained exceptionally high BET surface area $(1134~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . High mesopore volume was retained as well and the fabricated monolith displayed applicable pores for methane storage. Despite $\sim40\%$ decrease in Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of 3D printed monolith, it retained $48\%$ absolute gas uptake capacities in comparison to MOF powder. In addition to direct forming of MOF gel as printable ink, the MOF anchored on graphene oxide [28], oxidized cellulose [29], meth­ ylcellulose [30], bentonite clay [31], Pluronic F127 powder [27] are a good binder to form employable hybrid ink for building 3D printed monoliths and scaffold, including ZIF-67, ZIF-8, MIL-100 (Fe). In an intriguing case, polycaprolactone acted as the binder for 3D-printed MOF-based scaffold in a bio-medical application [32]. Powder bed fusion is another technique in 3D printing to manufacture MO∙F monolith [33]. Zhou group has made considerable achievements in 3D printing techniques. They expanded the application scope of 3D printing-selec­ tive laser sintering, which has important pioneering significance to treat MOF, and provide forward-looking strategies for fabrication of 3Dprinted MOF monolith [34]. These results achieved inspiring manufacturing approach as an extendable platform for more MOFs, but the recyclability and mechanical strength of the formulated MOF monolith should be systematically evaluated for industrial applications. To meet this requirement, Thakkar et al. tested mechanical strength of printed MOF monolith using Instron mechanical testing instrument, followed by calculation of compression strength and Young’s modulus [35]. The recorded compressive strengths before catastrophic failure for 3D-printed MOF-74(Ni) and UTSA-16(Co) monoliths were $0.48\mathrm{MPa}$ and $0.56\mathrm{MPa}$ , respectively, while Young’s modulus for these two monoliths were 12 and $25\mathrm{MPa}$ . This research opens a door for investigating me­ chanical properties, but still insufficient for industrial translation, thus more work needs to be accomplished in the future.
## 2.2. In-situ growth
MOFs can grow on appropriate supportive substrate, such as zinc oxide pallet [36], copper foam [37], polyurethane sponge [38], etc., facilitated by various motivation. Gu et al. built an integrated water–oil separator, in which polyurethane sponge played an important role in offering robust mechanical stability and excellent recyclability after 100 cycles. The decoration on polyurethane sponge of MOF-graphene com­ posite was facilitated by hydroxy- polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) cross­ linking. In this elegantly-designed architecture, superhydrophobic and superoleophilic properties are achieved via powerful synergistic enhancement from both ZIF-8 and wrinkled graphene-based micro­ spheres. The growth on specific substrate is assisted by binders such as polymers, cellulose and clay, et c. However, it increases the product cost and the potential bubbling may cause insufficient adherence, poor me­ chanical resistance and unsatisfactory stability. Moreover, the use of binders is suffering from poor conductivity and unfavorable for exposing adsorption sites and catalytically active sites. Resultantly, in-situ growth strategy is attractive due to the binder-free advantage. Briefly, the direct formation of MOF on the substrate was obtained by direct immersing in the precursor solution or MOF suspension under synthetic conditions [39]. Qi et al. [36] confirmed ZnMOF was capable of growing on $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ substrate for fabricating an antibody functionalized monolith for tumor cell recognizing and capture. The growth is promoted by the same metal node from substrate and MOF, which is consistent with more research [40–41].
3D foam is another good resource as a substrate. The highly porous structure of 3D foam of metals (i.e. Cu, Sn, Zr) renders it advantages in constructing complex architectures [74]. The 3D metal foam has selfsupported architecture derived from effective electrochemical deposi­ tion. More importantly, the pore size and wall density can be tunable, offering abundant modification sites of the internal surface. In the deformation mechanism study, it was confirmed that porous 3D foam behaved more like a solid metal homogeneously, providing good me­ chanical strength [75]. Therefore, this unique property is beneficial for surviving in extreme conditions as well as long-lasting recycling. Lin et al. [37] developed a decoration method using solvothermal sonicat­ ion. Before growth on the copper foam, a $12\mathrm{-mm}$ sized circular disk was cut from bulk copper foam and possible oxide layer was removed by treatment with acetone, hydrochloric acid, and distilled water, followed by dispersion of prepared ZIF-67 suspension in methanol. The sol­ vothermal sonication promoted the ZIF-67 decoration on the copper foam substrate. Adhesion of MOFs coating on the substrate is a key factor to evaluate coating quality and stability. Similarly, a $20\:\mathrm{mm}\times2\:\mathrm{mm}\times2\:\mathrm{mm}$ copper foam bar was ultrasonically pretreated by solvent and dried for follow-up loading in a Teflon-lined autoclave together with MOF-5 precursor [44]. $500~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ -thick MOF-5 was immo­ bilized on the copper foam bar with good adhesion as well as high sta­ bility for up to 200 replicated use.
A variety of MOFs powder (UiO-66, $\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , UiO-67, and HKUST-1) can be compressed as a 3D tablet, the bulk density is increased by 1.8–3.4-fold [68]. Meanwhile, when mild compression was applied (usually around $20\mathrm{MPa},$ , both bough and highly porous struc­ ture of tablet can be maintained for catalysis. But high pressure and moisture can cause collapse of fabricated tablets, therefore this approach is not applicable to all scenarios, especially in terms of gas storage, the requirement of good porosity is stricter.
As the layer-by-layer deposition of MOFs on substrate is timeconsuming and difficult to scale up, Arslan and coworkers developed a novel strategy to produce monolithic, oriented, crystalline and highly porous coatings on solid substrates [69]. Furthermore, with regard to applications such as gas storage and separation, homogenous coatings are necessarily required. This high-throughput route is based on liquid phase epitaxy (LPE), by which large deposition rate and structural quality are achieved for MOF growth. Briefly, an aerosol of MOFs reactant and solutions is produced in nozzles with a $10~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ -size. Sub­ sequently, the aerosol droplets impinge on the substrate to form a MOFcoated surface. Different from the rinsing process in LPE, it has been replaced in the spray method by substrate explosion to a produced aerosol from pure solvent, producing an oriented growth.
## 2.3. Casting
Casting is a facile method and applicable for both soft and hard substrates. For fabricating MOF-based microneedle (MN) system, a universal route is direct casting [78]. In general, a master mold should be designed firstly, which is used for making a silicon negative mold. Afterwards, materials for building MN patches are casted in MN pores with assistance of centrifuge. For instance, the Zn-MOF containing MN was formed by introducing methacrylated hyaluronic acid (MeHA) as the light-curing medium. Briefly, the as-synthesized ZIF-8 particles were mixed with MeHA in solvent at $\mathtt{p H}6$ . The mixture was loaded into a negative mold, then cured by UV irradiation $(365~\mathrm{nm})$ [46].
Recently, Zhang and coauthors suggested that MOFs drop-casted on a quartz glass $(7.5\ \mathrm{cm}\ \times\ 2.5\ \mathrm{cm})$ ) can be converted into MOF-derived carbon in designed patterns with the assistance of a $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ infrared laser system [45]. The laser energy induced MOFs into carbon, forming continuous network microstructure. This technique is beneficial for creating desired patterns and structures in MOF-monolith-related applications.
In addition to solid support, shape-transformable substrate is attracting great interest in various applications, including soft robot [79], flexible electronics [80], and wearable devices [81]. Compared to solid substrate, self-shaping merit renders the whole 3D architectures greater complexity and more flexibility. Consequently, 3D MOF-based system based on foldable substrate is a great choice. One successful example is accomplished by Troyano and co-authors [71]. They devel­ oped a MIL-88A@PVDF monolith with reversible $\ensuremath{2\mathrm{D}}\ \leftrightarrow\ 3\mathrm{D}$ trans­ formations, exhibiting a walking-like movement upon exposure to alternating UV–vis irradiation. This controllable movement provides possible potentials in soft MOF robot development. Another represen­ tative case is developed by Wang group on non-woven fabrics via direct deposition after MOF precursor was loaded on the substrate and heated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{min}$ .
## 2.4. Other strategies
Thermoplastic polyamide has been used universally to prepare MOFpolymer matrix, which can be fabricated as mixed MOFs filters [34]. The hot-melt MOF-polymer matrix is sintered using a program-controlled laser beam. In this process, voids are formed to increase the contact area when melt MOF-polymer powder is sintered controllably and rapidly by utilizing transient laser heating. Therefore, the voids and patterns can be designed and adjusted by changing laser sintering pa­ rameters, such as power, scanning speed, and hatching space. The advantage of this technique is scalability, extendibility and controlla­ bility, which is applied for ZIF-67, $\mathrm{{NH}}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Al), MOF-801, HKUST1, and ZIF-8, showing distinct superiority in large-scale manufacturing.
In some cases, the fabrication of MOF-derived monoliths is facing a complex challenge for combing each component. Thus, a comprehensive approach is needed for accomplish the set goals. The in-situ decoration on a 3D structure often relies on MOFs secondary growth, usually requiring an optimized seeding in the initial process of nanocrystals growth [61]. The uniform seeding, however, is tricky and challenging to be obtained, especially on the whole surface in a complicated 3D sup­ port. To overcome this drawback, alternatively, the on atom-by-atom reactive deposition enables the binding at atom scale to form a uni­ form precursor distribution and seeding, for homogeneous growth of MOFs. For example, in a toxic gas remover, a 3D-printed ABS module with disc geometry $(22~\mathrm{mm}\times3.2~\mathrm{mm})$ was employed as the substrate [63]. Subsequently, a step-by-step method was used by depositing $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ firstly, followed by conversion of $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ to ZIF-8 by adding 2-methyl imidazole solution, which was carried out in hydrothermal conditions. It’s worth noting that the ZIF-8 growth didn’t alter the stability of the polymeric ABS substrate.
Overall, the strategies for fabricating MOF-based 3D architecture are mainly divided into two categories. One is decoration on the surface of 3D monoliths, including in-situ growth, large-scale spray, and dropcasting. Direct deposition usually employs the substrate after appro­ priate cleaning and pretreatment as the support material for in-situ modification. It usually requires good seeding as the initial process of growth to enable subsequent growth, therefore, this technique is chal­ lenging especially on complicated 3D architectures. To address this issue, polymers are good binders in the decoration, i.e. PVDF, to endow good adhesion between MOFs and the substrate. It’s a universal approach for various MOFs, however, polymers may block the pores, channels and active sites in MOFs, unfavorable in catalysis, gas storage and other applications associated with MOFs pores. The spay is easy to scale up in manufacturing, especially in industrial applications. But universality of this approach is unsatisfactory. The stability of direct drop-casting needs to be scrutinized, thus atom-by-atom reactive deposition is more attractive for obtaining good binding. The other category for building MOF-mediated 3D monolith is based on 3D printing technique. The MOF particles or precursors are mixed with supportive materials (i.e. cellulose, PVA, clay) or thermoplastic polymer as matrix, then the mixture can be manipulated via printing techniques, such as extrusion 3D printing. Notably, it’s controllable, designable and large-scalable, which can be explored as a universal method for building various MOF monolith.
## 3. Applications
## 3.1. Energy storage
In the past decades, despite tremendous efforts have been devoted to developing advanced batteries, all-dimensional superiority in superhigh mass energy density, excellent volume energy density, and outstanding cycling performance is still facing huge challenge [82]. In response to this tricky issue, MOF-related materials and monoliths are of great value to be investigated. MOFs are employed in electrode and separator in energy storage and conversion, though high porosity is favorable to promote the overall electrochemical performance, both benefits and limitations coexists in this area [83]. The adjustable pores and tunable compositions of MOFs are advantageous in search of elec­ trode materials (both cathode [84–85] and anode [86–87]), but hinder conductivity with inferior electrochemical stability, even not compara­ ble to commercial materials [83]. Thus far, MOFs-derives supply blaze new trails in the field of energy storage, rather than direct utilization. MOFs can serve as self-sacrificial templates for preparing various elec­ trode materials, especially in 3D-supportive structures, which demon­ strated improved electrochemical behaviors owing to enhanced stability and electrical conductivity.
The growth on various 3D conductive substrates, i.e. copper foam/ reduced graphene oxide (Fig. 2(a)) [52], 3D graphene [54], allows to fabricate binder-free $\mathrm{Cu}_{0.39}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.14}\mathrm{Co}_{2.47}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-ZnO}$ and $\mathrm{CuO}/3\mathrm{D}$ graphene network, respectively, exhibiting an enhanced lithium storage perfor­ mance. Notably, the rate capability was overwhelmingly improved compared to their counterparts without 3D conductive substrates [67], benefitting from greatly facilitated electron/ion transport in the highly porous 3D conductive structure. Besides, the constructed carbon aerogel derived from MOF-5 cylinder exhibits unprecedented high surface area $(1916~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ with high porosity, good compressibility, as well excel­ lent conductivity, which resulted in high initial capacity (3100 $\mathbf{mAh}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}\mathbf{\cdot}$ ) and prolonged cycling life for lithium-ion batteries. Impor­ tantly, the rich metal species derived from MOFs provide catalytic sites for the oxygen reduction reaction in $\mathbf{Li-O}_{2}$ batteries, obtaining superior performance compared to that of counterparts cathodes (Fig. 2(b)) [27,88]. The initial 3D MOF-derived strategy provides important in­ sights into the design of advanced electrode materials extended from Aucoated copper foam to flexible substrate for constructing flexible bat­ teries in the future [37].
From a different point of view, MOFs-decorated separator has attracted great interests. It was reported that in lithium-sulfur batteries, the MOF-based separator acts as an ionic sieve, which selectively filters ${\bf\Delta}{\bf{{L i}}}^{+}$ ions while efficiently muffling undesired polysulfides shuttling to the anode side (Fig. 2(c)) [57–58].
In addition to Li-related batteries, the carbonized MOF-monolith has demonstrated to perform as 12-interdigitated electrodes for electro­ chemical energy storage applications, using MOF-199@ZIF-67 with core–shell structure [45]. Resultantly, high-performance in specific capacitance was achieved from as-obtained hierarchical electrodes comprising mesoporous structure and interlaced network.
Although MOF-based monoliths hold exciting promise in perspective e of higher rechargeable capability, better rate capacity and improved cycling performance compared to commercial materials, the industrial translation is hampered by the following issues: (a) the inherent highly porous structure results in low volumetric energy density; (b) the syn­ thesis of MOFs is costly and energy-consuming (i.e. high-temperature annealing), impeding large-scale production and application in prac­ tical; (c) the carbon component derived from MOFs may suffer from decomposition, leading to instability and poor cycling stability. After these aforementioned issues carefully considered and addressed, the translation in industry will be more practical for wide utilization.
## 3.2. Separation
In terms of adsorptive separation, numerous researches have vali­ dated good performance in waste water treatment [89], precious metal recovery [90], target molecules capture [91] and toxic molecules removal [92]. Similar to other applications in the form of powder morphology, the drawback lies in chemical and mechanical instability, making it difficult to be recycled, especially in large-scale applications.
To overcome these disadvantages, Lefevere and coworkers developed a 3D-printed ZIF-8 monolith to improve the scalability of MOFs with successful separation in an isopropanol-butanol-ethanol model mixture, showing good capacity and selectivity for n-butanol [30]. For industrial applications, the assessment of their reusability and mechanical stiffness in a cyclic process should still be evaluated. Likewise, based on adsorption mechanism, the 3D printed MOF monolith can be extended for toxic gas removal [63], water purification (Fig. 3(c)) [34,70] and volatile organic compound capture [93]. 3D printed Cu-BET within biocompatible polymers in various geometry can be utilized as recy­ clable and scalable adsorbents for a broad organic dyes removal [48]. Unfortunately, the adsorption sites of MOFs being embedded in the binders, such as clay and polymers, are prone to be blocked, leading to adsorption capacity inhibition. In-situ growth on the 3D skeleton can consolidate MOFs powders into a monolith, which is an alternative approach. Reusability is an important fact to evaluate practicality in industry, so 3–7 consecutive tests were investigated for MOF-mediated 3D skeletons [34,48,53,70]. Although authors claimed excellent reusability was obtained, it’s far from enough for industrial applications. 100 recycling tests were examined in only a few examples (Fig. 3(e [38])). Thereby, more efforts should be devoted to improving recycla­ bility as well as reducing cost remarkably to meet the requirements for better conversion in practical applications.
## 3.3. Gas storage
Due to the mechanical instability, poor handling properties and recycling difficulty of the polydisperse crystalline powders [14,94], which is the main form of as-synthesized MOFs samples, in the past decade, the MOF-based monolith has gained considerable attention. To date, the monolithic MOF has been explored in various applications, one of which is aiming at gas storage. Despite record-breaking gas-storage properties of MOFs, the powdered morphology limits their industrial applications [66]. Compared with conventional powder form, the integrity of MOF monolith gives it better manufacturability and superiority [94–95], and interestingly, with comparable gas adsorption capacity to MOF powder [47]. A 3D-printed polymer-composite mono­ liths was fabricated by Thakkar et al. with $\sim1\mathrm{cm}$ in diameter and $2\mathrm{cm}$ in height. The obtained 3D-printed monoliths showed enhanced me­ chanical strength and textural properties for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption [35]. The binder assisted 3D printing technique makes MOFs possible to be printed as a 3D monolith [29–31], resultantly, the gas separation/storage can be achieved but the inevitable decrease in surface area and porosity poses risks in reduce of storage capacity. Ideally, the obtained monolithic MOF maintains accessible porosity with retention of gas storage capability. An exciting example reported by Wheatley group overcomes collapse in porosity or low-density structure using an engineered UiO-66 block without applying pressure or binders [66]. Another case showed even better results than expectation [13]. High-density gas storage was verified by introducing gas condensation in wider cavities at higher pressures, showing transition from Type I isotherm to Type $\mathrm{~I~+~IV~}$ isotherm, typically derived from mesopores. These results pave roads to developing industry-targeting porous and regenerative metal–organic framework monolith. Despite the above-mentioned successful trials, binders in monolithic MOFs fabrication, especially in 3D printing, are like a double-edged sword to enhance manufacturability and decreased porosity. Therefore, a wise balance should be made in the future to make more advanced MOF-based gas-storage monolith.
## 3.4. Bio-medical applications
As MOFs are constructed by metal ions and organic linkers, the porous nature enabled them to accommodate therapeutic cargos for drug delivery. For realizing biomedical purposes with good clinical transnationality, biocompatibility and biodegradability are key points to be considered carefully. It’s worth noting that the biodegradability and negligible cytotoxicity of several MOFs has been verified by tremendous research, such as ZIF-8 [96], making them good drug carriers. With controllable and stimuli-responsive release [97]. However, lack of motile features makes MOFs particles incompetent to make active movement in the human body (i.e. gastrointestinal tract). The advan­ tages of small-scale biomedical device is locomotion capabilities can be endowed via magnetic field [98], deformable structure design [99–100], ejection force [101], etc. A novel example of MOF-mediated biomedical device, denoted as artificial bacterial flagella with helical structure, was fabricated by Wang and coworkers [42], which was coated by nickel and titanium to make it magnetically responsive, followed by MOF crystals grew on the surface to load cancer drugs for drug delivery. Note that the composite monolith can swim and rotate synchronously with weak external magnetic field with a corkscrew locomotion. Therefore, the responsive motion facilitated by magnetic properties of MOF-monolith and drug delivery capability are an integrated multifunctional system, composing the first microrobot based on MOFs for drug delivery. The invivo evaluation of this MOFBOT should be investigated in the future to obtain more feasibility. In their MOFBOTs version 2.0, they developed a highly integrated MOFBOTs with capability of magnetic locomotion, drug delivery and selective degradation in cell cultures, moving forward from proof-of-concept study to close-to-clinical research by employing enzymatically biodegradable gelatin as the microrobot core.
Beyond movement, MOF-monolith in our lab provides feasibility of cancer cell capture and drug delivery (Fig. 4(a)) [36]. The recognition of cells can be achieved by modification of anti-epithelial cell adhesion molecule (EpCAM) antibody and further enhanced by pseudopodia bi­ onic morphology. Besides, as MOFs is competent to load therapeutic cargos and release drug in a responsive manner, the fabricated ZnMOFbased platform acted as an efficient attacker to deliver drug cargos and realize controllable release in response to external stimuli after tumor cell capture. Thus, the fabricated MOF-based immunotrapper success­ fully combined these two fields and exhibited enormous potential in the development of personalized therapy.
Microneedle (MN) patches have been a hot micro-device for versatile applications in a minimally invasive manner, including vaccination [102], cancer therapy [103], cosmetic [104], et c. Efforts have been made by several groups to build a novel type of MOF-based MN patches to promote wound healing [104], and drug delivery (Fig. 4(b)) [46]. The fabricated ZIF-8 MN exhibited antibacterial and tissue regeneration capability, offering a promising strategy for wound healing, attributed to sustainable release of zinc ions to induce the destruction of bacteria metabolism and membrane structure. Furthermore, the synergistic ef­ fects to kill bacteria come from hyaluronic acid derived from MeHA, to promote angiogenesis and reduce inflammation in the wounds. Since it’s crucial for MN to penetrate into tissue, the mechanical of MOF-based MN patches were investigated, illustrating efficient robustness for skin penetration (Fig. 4(d-f)) [46,73]. In addition to the therapeutic effects coming from MOFs in response to infection and inflammatory, admin­ istrable drug release from MOF MN can be easily accomplished, benefitted from the good drug-bearing capability; for example, ZIF-8 MN arrays were explored for transdermal insulin delivery [46], which is promising to be improved as an advanced platform with better perfor­ mance to achieve glucose-responsive insulin delivery, since responsive formulation based on ZIF-8 particles has been successfully developed [105].
During COVID-19 pandemic, masks have been recognized as neces­ sities of life to some extent [106]. Surgical masks stop liquid splash and reduce virus spread from infected persons by offering a filtering barrier. Beyond this, masks incorporated functional molecules or materials are designed to target more demands; for example, the ZIF-8-loaded nonwoven fabrics can be applied for making MOFilter masks with photo­ catalytic bactericidal performances, exhibiting nearly complete inacti­ vation efficacy for killing gram-negative E. coli, facilitated by producing reactive oxygen species through a photodynamic process (Fig. 4(g)) [49]. This work makes MOF-related masks an important medical supply and extendable for developing multi-functional masks.
To satisfy all requirements for clinical translation, research efforts should be further devoted to evaluating systematically not only biocompatibility, therapeutic loading capacity, delivery efficiency, but also monitoring/ tracking, biodegradability and in-vivo clearance. So new paradigms in materials design and fabrication in monoliths are of equal significance to integrate all these features into a single biomedical device.
## 3.5. Catalysis
In chemical industry applications, especially heterogeneous catal­ ysis, MOFs exhibit great significance due to ultrahigh surface area, tunable topology and inherent large porosity [6]. Rownaghi group verified the feasibility of preparing monolithic catalysts through 3D printing technology which is a rapid, cost-efficient way of manufacture [95,107]. Moreover, the 3D-printed structure facilitated selectivity to some extent [108]. Likewise, the formation of MOFs into monolithic or pellet structure is designed to break the limitations in catalysis caused by the rigidity and fragility of MOFs. The key points to maintain good catalytic performance are: (a) retained good porosity; (b) favored mass transfer; (c) stability. Taking all these into consideration.
To avoid block of catalytic sites caused by binder, the direct growth of MIL-100 (Fe) was achieved on a 3D-printed porous substrate via hy­ drothermal reaction, reported by Liu et al. [64]. Resultantly, the 3D-sup­ ported catalysts with hierarchical pores were enabled to completely decompose organic dyes. The highest removal efficiency can reach up to $99.68\%$ with degradation rate $(K_{o b s})$ $\cdot2915\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , ascribed to large surface area, abundant active sites, and hierarchical transport channels. More importantly, 50 effective reusable cycles were accomplished with over $75\%$ degradation efficiency, holding great value in practical waste water treatment.
MOF-monolith derivatives also demonstrated ignorant values in electrocatalysts and photocatalysts [39,55,109]. Efficient catalysts are essential for achieving green sustainable energy transformation. MOF derivatives are candidates for precious-metal-based catalysts due to their unique structures, which have great potential application in energy conversion (water splitting, oxygen reduction reactions, carbon dioxide reduction reaction, etc.) [110]. To date, several excellent resultes have been published on the design and application of MOF derivatives in energy conversion. Carbonization of MOFs on conductive substrates using the laser-induced annealing was achieved by Tang et al., after­ wards the assembled two-electrode electrolyzer exhibited superior water splitting performance [111]. In another typical example, MOFs insitu grown on Ni foam without use of binders was employed as a sacrificial template via annealing. The resultant product denoted as FeNiCo@NC/NF-T performed well in hydrogen/oxygen evolution, benefited from the superior electron transports channels and reduced internal impedance from self-supportive 3D structure in the conductive Ni foam substrate. Furthermore, Mo et al. proposed that synergistic ef­ fects from higher specific surface area and lower-temperature reduc­ ibility resulted from unique hierarchical structure enhanced the catalytic activities. However, as at least five-step synthesis routes are needed to prepare 3D MOF-based derivatives, it’s both time-consuming and energy-consuming, leading to much more cost in practical appli­ cations. Another issue is the complexity in morphology and structure limits the scalability in practical, for example the leaf-like Co-ZIF-L de­ rivatives [50], thus introducing huge troubles and uncertainty in cata­ lyst preparation with good quality control.
## 3.6. Motors
Recently, integrating the monolith with movement and good control has gained much attention from researchers. Motion of the system can be controlled magnetically [112], catalytically motivated [113] and light driven [114]. The MOF-mediated motors harness exogenous or integrated energy for locomotion, in order to implement refined and advanced missions, such as trackable therapeutics. The adsorbed pay­ loads in MOFs are released in a highly ordered stream, thereby the MOFs move towards higher surface tension and the motion can be stopped by adding hydrophobic molecules without self-assembling capability [115]. Initially, the self-driven motion was investigated in MOFs parti­ cles as proof-of-concept studies [116–117]. With more efforts being put in this domain, it turns to study MOF-monolith for seeking possibility. Different-shaped MOF pieces exhibited various motion styles (Fig. 5(ad)). For example, circular disks move randomly whereas slanted rect­ angular bars perform rapid rotation [62]. The first MOF robot, denoted as MOFBOT, was developed by Wang and coworkers (Fig. 5(e-g)) [42]. The MOF-based motor was fabricated layer by layer on a helical poly­ meric chassis with Ni/Ti coating. Upon weak magnetic field applied, the MOFBOT can move in the predesigned direction. The proposed device is designed to target single cells for subsequent pH-responsive drug delivery in a microfluidic channel. This new approach offers new ave­ nues to build self-motivated MOF-monoliths in various applications. In recent progress, MOFBOT has been endowed both magnetically activity and biodegradability [118]. In addition to locomotion, the deformation of MOF-monoliths is an alternative strategy (Fig. 5(h)) [71–72]. The programmable architecture was based on swellable MIL-88A, which is able to swell upon exposure to moisture and shrink under UV–vis light. Reversible 2D-to-3D shape transformations, including walking-like movement, self-folding and scrolling. The programmability and controllability of this system is favorable for micromanipulation, auto­ mation, and robotics. Furthermore, the combination of two motion mechanism was achieved in a ZIF-8 motor system, which was magnet­ ically responsive and bubble-propelled at a high speed of ca. $860\pm230~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}{\cdot}s^{-1}$ [56]. This system is desirable in self-propelled scav­ enger for radioactive wastewater treatment. Up to now, study on 3D MOF motors is still not widely reported and facing huge challenges in precise control, endurance, and regeneration. After addressing these issues, the integrated system is promising to be utilized in various domains in the near future, such as in-vivo biomedical robots, smart surgical devices, automated wastewater treatment and other practical applications.
## 3.7. Luminescent sensing
MOF-monoliths are very promising candidates for luminescent sensing, as they show luminescence properties with multiple mecha­ nisms, structural advantages and the ability to selectively identify some specific substances. Among them, lanthanides are widely used in the luminescence sensing research of MOF monolith due to their unique photophysical properties. Zhou et al. synthesized a bimetallic lanthanide organic framework with self-calibrating properties using $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ and furan-2,5-dicarboxylic acid [119]. Zhao group expanded the $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ ratio, and provided a promising strategy for luminescent sensing of different antibiotics utilizing luminescent responses [120]. In addition, 3D-printed luminescent MOF monolith holds great significance for designing luminescent sensing platform [121]. The mechanism by which ligand-based luminescent MOFs are used for luminescent sensing is quenching by guest molecules in most applications. Qian group uti­ lized $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ , organic dye and MOF-253 to develop a luminescent sensing array that generates multiple-dimensional luminescence to distinguish multiple metal ions, which is a typical application of fluorescence quenching [122].
Although MOF-based luminescent sensing platform has been applied to detect ions [123], volatile organic compounds [124], explosives [125], and biomolecules [126], the research on luminescent sensing still faces great challenges in anti-interference evaluation, real-time moni­ toring, and processability of fabricated devices, especially in the development of portable devices.
## 4. Conclusions and outlook
Up to now, according to published results, over 90,000 MOFs have been identified and characterized with detailed structural information around the world [13]. Numerous MOFs allow for significant potential in industrial and clinical applications, benefitted from their unique chemical and physical properties. However, the applicability should be enhanced in various fields, such as industrial catalysis, high-density gasstorage and biomedical applications, which is because the full potential is far from being achieved and their application scenario remains limited when large-scalability, high-density, good-controllability and excellentrecyclability is strictly required. The MOF-based 3D monolith is trying to address the aforementioned issues from a new perspective, and corre­ sponding advantages are illustrated in Fig. 6. The MOF-monoliths sur­ pass MOF powder for supplying more sites for guest molecules created by monolithic structures. Improved mechanical strength and locomotion can be obtained with assistance from substrate-mediated 3D compo­ nents (Fig. 6). Inspiring progress has been made by researcher to achieve over 100 recycling in oil–water separation [38]. Higher gas storage capacity is achieved by additional mesopores in the MOF monolith.
Despite considerable efforts have been made, challenges are still remained:
1) The construction of MOF-monolith leads to inevitable decrease in surface area and adsorption capacity to some extent, especially when binder was used in the process of fabrication, thereby reducing the accessibility of MOF pores to guest molecules [26,47]. This issue can be addressed by no-binder fabrication strategies [55–57,118].   
2) The fabrication method of in-situ growth constrains the total MOF loading on substrate. Therefore, the monolithic properties, porosity and stability should be balanced well. As precise 3D printing tech­ nology is developing dramatically, it may provide a better solution to balance monolithic properties, porosity and stability. Ideally, the 3D printed MOF-monolith with good integrity and good porosity remained to meet all-dimensional requirements for obtaining stateof-the-art performance.   
3) The pore-dependent applications, such as gas storage and separation [127], requires natural porosity retained to maintain high adsorption capability. The best situation is that the constructed MOF-monolith can supply additional pores during the process of fabrication, as exampled in the published results [13].
Undoubtedly, this area is far from discovered and developed. Advanced fabrication methods and more application cases are to be explored in the near future. In perspective of biomedical applications, the biosafety evaluation should be dived in depth and make a compre­ hensive understanding in toxicity and in-vivo metabolic routes. Good biocompatibility and biodegradability should be guaranteed for enhanced clinical transnationality.
